{"term":"transa","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":35,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Informal noun and verb for sex or a sexual encounter; mild slang equivalent of 'a lay' or 'a roll in the hay.'","english_term":"a lay","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The verb form is 'transar' (to have sex); also means a shady deal or dubious scheme in non-sexual contexts — the overlap between sex and a dodgy transaction is semantically interesting; one of the most versatile and widely used mild sex terms in Brazilian Portuguese."}
{"term":"trepa","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":50,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":1990,"regional":null,"definition":"Noun for a sexual encounter; from 'trepar' (to climb, to have sex); somewhat dated informal slang for a fuck.","english_term":"a shag","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The verb 'trepar' remains more common than the noun 'trepa'; the term has faded relative to 'transa' and 'foda' among younger speakers; peak usage was in the 1980s–90s; 'trepar' literally means to climb, giving the word a physical/athletic connotation."}
{"term":"ferrando","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_crude","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Gerund of 'ferrar' (to brand with iron, to screw over); used crudely to mean 'fucking,' with connotations of vigorous or forceful sex.","english_term":"screwing","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Ferrar' has primary meanings of 'to shoe a horse' and 'to brand with iron'; the sexual meaning derives from notions of hard, forceful contact; less universal than 'fodendo' (gerund of foder); may be perceived as more aggressive or violent in connotation."}
{"term":"dando uma de","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_implied","concept":"intercourse_implied","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Idiomatic phrase meaning 'acting as / doing the role of [sexual partner]'; functions as a circumlocution implying sexual intercourse, often with undertones of the receptive role.","english_term":"doing it","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Incomplete phrase requiring context; 'dar' in Brazilian sexual slang specifically means to be the receptive partner in sex (giving one's body); 'dando uma de' can therefore imply performing the receptive role; deeply context-dependent and not a fixed sexual term on its own."}
{"term":"fazer aquilo","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_implied","concept":"intercourse_implied","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":8,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'to do that thing'; a polite euphemism for sexual intercourse, avoiding direct naming of the act through deictic reference.","english_term":"to do the deed","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Aquilo' (that thing) functions as a placeholder for the unspoken sexual act; universally understood in context; used when speakers wish to imply sex without stating it, often in front of children or in mixed-formality settings; the same deictic strategy appears across most world languages."}
{"term":"meter o pau","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"piv_intercourse","concept":"piv_intercourse","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'to put the stick in'; crude phrasal verb for penile penetration and sexual intercourse, equivalent to 'to stick it in.'","english_term":"to stick it in","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Pau' (stick/wood) is one of the most common Brazilian slang terms for penis; the phrase explicitly foregrounds penetration as the defining act; applicable to vaginal or anal penetration depending on context; 'meter' (to put in/insert) carries a stronger sense of thrusting than 'enfiar'."}
{"term":"dar uma fodida","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_crude","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":80,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'to give a fuck'; crude phrase for having a casual sexual encounter; the diminutive 'fodida' maintains crudeness while slightly softening the register.","english_term":"to have a fuck","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Foda' is the noun form of 'foder' (to fuck); 'fodida' is a diminutive/casual variant suggesting a casual or opportunistic encounter; does not specifically imply brevity the way 'rapidinha' does — the diminutive here signals informality rather than speed."}
{"term":"uma rapidinha","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"quickie_sex","concept":"quickie_sex","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":25,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'a quick one'; the standard Brazilian Portuguese idiom for a quickie — a brief, hasty sexual encounter.","english_term":"a quickie","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Rapidinha' is a feminine diminutive of 'rápida' (quick, fast); very widely used across all regions and demographics; relatively mild and euphemistic despite its unambiguous sexual meaning; the diminutive suffix softens the crudeness while also suggesting something furtive or stolen."}
