{"term":"fellatio","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"blowjob","concept":"fellatio","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":5,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Unmutated Latin term for oral stimulation of the penis; encountered in Brazilian legal, medical, and academic texts alongside the Portuguese adaptation 'felação.'","english_term":"fellatio","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used interchangeably with 'felação' in some formal and academic Brazilian contexts; international medical literature read by Brazilian professionals often retains the Latin form; lower recognition than 'felação' because the Portuguesized spelling is more commonly taught."}
{"term":"cunilíngua","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"cunnilingus","concept":"cunnilingus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical Brazilian Portuguese term for cunnilingus; oral stimulation of the vulva.","english_term":"cunnilingus","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The standard medical and legal designation for this act in Brazilian Portuguese; much lower recognition than crude equivalents; appears primarily in clinical texts, legal documents, and sex education materials; the lower recognition compared to 'felação' likely reflects historical under-discussion of female pleasure in public health contexts."}
{"term":"cunnilingus","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"cunnilingus","concept":"cunnilingus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":5,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Unmutated Latin term for oral stimulation of the vulva; used in Brazilian medical, legal, and academic texts alongside the Portuguese adaptation 'cunilíngua.'","english_term":"cunnilingus","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Sometimes used interchangeably with 'cunilíngua' in formal Brazilian texts; Brazilian recognition is lower than in English-speaking countries because English-language sex education and media have made the Latin form familiar to English speakers in a way that Portuguese has not replicated."}
{"term":"estimulação oral","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"acts","subcategory":"oral_sex_generic","concept":"oral_sex_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":3,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'oral stimulation'; a clinical descriptor for any oral-to-genital sexual contact, encompassing both fellatio and cunnilingus.","english_term":"oral stimulation","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Preferred in medical and public-health contexts for its gender-neutral and act-neutral framing; appears in Brazilian HIV-prevention literature and sexual health guidelines; the act-neutral quality makes it useful when the specific genital involved need not be specified."}
